# Variable NAMES only. Never a value — not a placeholder that looks real, not a
# development key, not a "safe" one. This file is tracked; anything written here
# is published.
#
# Real values live in two places: Vercel, where every one is created as
# Sensitive, and an untracked `.env.local` on the maintainer's machine. The
# rules are in .claude/context/security-auth.md; the list below is kept beside
# the decisions that introduce each variable, in .claude/context/backend.md.
#
# No code reads any of these yet. `GET /api/health`, the only route as of plan
# batch 4.2, reads no secret at all — it reports the deployment's commit and
# region from Vercel's own public system variables.
#
# Present on the maintainer's machine since plan batch 5.0, which linked the
# Neon project. The Neon CLI owns these three lines in `.env.local` — both
# `neon deploy` and `neon env pull` rewrite them — but it merges rather than
# replaces, so every other line in that file survives. The Phase 5 names below
# are therefore hand-written into the same file when their batch arrives.
# `db/index.ts` reads DATABASE_URL from plan batch 5.1a, and drizzle-kit reads
# DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED — the pooled host runs PgBouncer in transaction mode and
# cannot run a migration. Neither is needed to work on the project: with
# DATABASE_URL unset, `npm run serve` and the whole test suite use an in-process
# PGlite database instead, so a fresh clone and CI need no account and no
# secret. NEON_BRANCH is read by nothing; it records which branch the two
# values point at.
#   DATABASE_URL
#   DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED
#   NEON_BRANCH             # which Neon branch the two values above point at
#
# Read by the API from plan batch 5.2a. None is needed to work on the project:
# with them unset the auth routes still mount, and `scripts/serve.mjs` opens the
# test-only e-mail-and-password door so the suite can hold a session without
# Google. Real Google sign-in needs all of them, and works on production and on
# localhost only — Google forbids wildcard redirect URIs, so a preview's
# changing URL can never be registered.
#
# ALLOWED_EMAILS is comma-separated and is the whole access-control boundary.
# An empty or missing value admits NOBODY, deliberately: locking the owner out
# is a complaint within the minute, and admitting anyone with a Google account
# is a thing nobody notices.
#   BETTER_AUTH_SECRET
#   BETTER_AUTH_URL
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
#   ALLOWED_EMAILS
#   AUTH_TEST_MODE          # local only, never set on a deployment
#
# Arriving with Neon Auth (plan batch 5.6). Not a secret — it is the public
# base URL of the Neon Auth service for this branch, and the allowlist webhook
# fetches Neon's signing keys from `<it>/.well-known/jwks.json` to check that a
# request really came from Neon. Read it with `neon neon-auth status`.
# Without it the webhook refuses every sign-up, which is the intended direction
# to fail:
#   NEON_AUTH_BASE_URL
